#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9c2ec6a-e04a-5e97-a91c-3a232e9aa29d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality or Unit Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a00323d-f93f-5304-838e-9a2020861786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520051} {:answer/id #uuid "2f593f76-c5a7-50c8-9b17-9fcace1dabaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{6}{5}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520063} {:answer/id #uuid "3da9c120-60eb-589b-a9aa-5cbfc90e5257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520078} {:answer/id #uuid "9340f66f-9d6d-54b6-808d-a0bf629f8943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{7}{3}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520202} {:answer/id #uuid "b27a859b-f70f-5a32-ba4f-97460ef30f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{5}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a00323d-f93f-5304-838e-9a2020861786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520051} :answer-field/id #uuid "0752f685-6167-5321-9403-2b36429faec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5876a38-e0d7-5724-8ef9-3dbbcd7f0e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-163005" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4df58d2f35cc69e8a567a65bc64f24e5da8820e1be5d229979b08fe2c3d1cd8f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in months) since Mary's baby was born and the number $y$ of centimeters he has grown. What is the baby's unit rate of growth?" :question/worked-solution "The baby's unit rate of growth in centimeters per month is simply the constant of proportionality for the given proportional relationship.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(2,5).$\n\n![](images/5b/5bbc6c5b4188c5db894ceebe7d347c901afb428d5d102864a90218ef53420a60.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary's baby's unit rate of growth is $\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5462223e-a206-572a-846a-378621922011" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Direct Variation Equation from a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acc6262-ca31-5c5f-ac0d-1ae4e682b14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520105} {:answer/id #uuid "a9466f4c-419a-5b13-82db-f0fde5671554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520223} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4e5346-6676-5c14-868d-660ee8405b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520283} {:answer/id #uuid "e5402f53-c6f1-5c01-821d-49754b58179c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520325} {:answer/id #uuid "f98ff76b-d478-5a9c-a544-9213463eb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98ff76b-d478-5a9c-a544-9213463eb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520356} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bcc8a79-2736-57a1-bfc2-56ec9e252477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "878ffbea-7b4e-53ce-97b4-dbbab3af9a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51829" :question/problem "![](images/81/815e7943ff4f01bf9a89cd6bb1214cf9266c8ae528cfd53e05a7ac56cf1e0e74.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $y$ and $x.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies directly with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=kx.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $y=8$ and $x=2.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =k\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{2} & =k \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\ny=4x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5462223e-a206-572a-846a-378621922011" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Direct Variation Equation from a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd7620a-2e0a-5048-84e8-580582cc4050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520060} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1e780f-72c4-55fe-86bf-f63cbb6742d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520134} {:answer/id #uuid "67143172-5c75-57e2-9507-5548168537b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520148} {:answer/id #uuid "8052a59d-160b-523d-93b3-2be518aa22c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520175} {:answer/id #uuid "faaf0326-bcd6-5fdb-9b82-fb8c891b09c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8052a59d-160b-523d-93b3-2be518aa22c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520175} :answer-field/id #uuid "df70821b-247b-5776-8c45-aa8d80611751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "817c5709-21ea-5c46-9e06-c595f5917c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-68204" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a5313995e9740a61b455a9c368aca6223a80c8bbea659f06ec2a63c8260747b.png)\n\nKaren decides to walk from her workplace to her house. There is a proportional relationship between the distance $x$ (in kilometers) she covers and the time $y$ (in hours) it takes her to cover that distance.\n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,1).$\n\n![](images/be/bec01f9dc01b340075f09dd459571fe8199116f18a6ab912eb958c7a6f9ee4f2.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$"}]}
